  "id": "DrugBank_DB00420 - MONDO_2258",
  "verbalization": "Promazine is implicated in the pathogenesis of pharyngitis through a series of indirect pathways, primarily via its indications for common cold and atopic conjunctivitis. The evidence suggests that Promazine may exert a therapeutic effect on pharyngitis through mechanisms that involve the treatment of underlying conditions such as common cold or atopic conjunctivitis. This is supported by the presence of intermediate entities—Bromodiphenhydramine and Chlorcyclizine—that link Promazine to pharyngitis via their own indications. While the direct causal relationship between Promazine and pharyngitis remains unestablished, the cumulative evidence points to a potential role of Promazine in the management of pharyngitis through its involvement in treating related conditions. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of considering the broader clinical context in which Promazine is used, as its effects may be mediated through the treatment of associated illnesses.",
